.TH LEX 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lex \- generate a lexical analyzer in C
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lex
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-n | \-v ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lex
reads a lexical-analyzer specification from each
.I file
in turn, as if they were one file, or from standard input if no
.I file
is given; a
.I file
of
.B \-
also means standard input.
It compiles the patterns into a single deterministic finite automaton
and writes a C scanner to
.I lex.yy.c
in the current directory, or to standard output with
.BR \-t .
.PP
This is an in-tree implementation that covers a subset of POSIX
.BR lex ;
see
.B BUGS
before relying on it.
.SS Input format
The input has a definitions section, a rules section and an optional
user-subroutines section, separated by lines beginning with
.BR %% .
The first
.B %%
is required.
.PP
In the definitions section:
.TP
.B %{ ... %}
Lines between a
.B %{
line and a
.B %}
line are copied to the output.
.TP
.I indented line
A line beginning with a blank or tab is copied to the output.
.TP
.I "name definition"
Defines a substitution used as
.BI { name }
in patterns.
.TP
.BI %s " name ..."
.PD 0
.TP
.BI %x " name ..."
.PD
Declare inclusive and exclusive start conditions.
.TP
.BR %array ", " %pointer
Make
.B yytext
an array of 65536 bytes, or a pointer into the input buffer (the
default).
.TP
.BR %p ", " %n ", " %a ", " %e ", " %k ", " %o
Table-size declarations.
They are accepted and ignored.
.PP
Other lines beginning with
.B %
(such as
.BR %option )
are silently ignored.
.PP
In the rules section, each rule is a pattern starting in the first
column, followed by blanks and an action.
The action is either a C block in braces, which may span lines, the
rest of the line as a single statement, or
.BR | .
A rule with no action copies the matched text to
.BR yyout .
A pattern may be prefixed by
.BI < sc1 , sc2 ,...>
to restrict it to those start conditions, and
.BI < sc >{
starts a block, ended by a line consisting of
.BR } ,
in which every rule gets that prefix.
Blank lines are skipped, and indented lines are discarded.
.PP
Everything after the second
.B %%
is copied to the end of the output.
.SS Patterns
Supported: ordinary characters;
.B .
(any byte except newline and NUL);
bracket expressions
.BI [ ... ]
and
.BI [^ ... ]
with ranges;
quoted strings
.BI \(dq ... \(dq ;
.BI { name }
substitutions; grouping with parentheses; alternation
.BR | ;
the operators
.BR * ,
.BR + ,
.B ?
and intervals
.BI { m } ,
.BI { m ,}
and
.BI { m , n } ;
a leading
.B ^
and a trailing
.BR $ ;
trailing context
.IB r / s ;
and the escapes
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \ev ,
.BR \e\e ,
.BI \e ooo
(octal),
.BI \ex hh
(hexadecimal) and
.BI \ec X
(control character).
Any other escaped character stands for itself.
Bracket expressions do not support character classes such as
.BR [:alpha:] .
.SS Generated scanner
The output defines
.BR yylex (),
.BR yywrap ()
(which always returns 1),
.BR yymore (),
.BR input (),
.BR unput (),
the macros
.BR ECHO ,
.BR BEGIN ,
.B REJECT
and
.BR yyless (),
and the variables
.BR yytext ,
.BR yyleng ,
.B yyin
and
.BR yyout ,
which default to standard input and standard output.
Start conditions are
.B #define
constants, with
.B INITIAL
equal to 0.
The longest match wins, and among matches of equal length the rule
that appears first.
Input that no rule matches is copied to
.BR yyout .
.PP
The output does not contain
.BR main ()
and there is no
.B \-ll
library; the program must supply its own
.BR main ().
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t
Write the scanner to standard output instead of
.IR lex.yy.c .
.TP
.B \-n
Do not print the statistics line.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-v
Print a
.BI "Statistics: " n " DFA states"
line to standard output after generating the scanner.
.PP
If both
.B \-n
and
.B \-v
are given, the last one wins.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The scanner was written.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option was given, an input file could not be opened, the
input contained a NUL byte, the
.B %%
ending the definitions section or a closing
.B %}
was missing, a pattern could not be compiled or used an undefined
.BI { name } ,
there were no rules, or
.I lex.yy.c
could not be created.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I lex.yy.c
Default output file.
.SH EXAMPLE
Count the lines of standard input:
.PP
.EX
$ cat count.l
%{
int lines;
%}
%%
\en      { lines++; }
\&.       ;
%%
int main(void) { yylex(); printf("%d\en", lines); return 0; }
$ lex count.l && cc \-o count lex.yy.c
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yacc (1),
.BR cc (1),
.BR yylex (3)
.SH STANDARDS
Partially implements
.B lex
as specified in POSIX.1-2024.
.SH BUGS
Progress and debugging messages (lines beginning with
.BR Mock: ,
the rule and definition dumps, the DFA state counts and
.BR "Generated lex.yy.c" )
are always written to standard output.
With
.B \-t
they are mixed into the generated scanner, which then does not
compile.
The
.B \-v
statistics line also goes to standard output.
.PP
Trailing context is not separated from the match:
.IB r / s
matches
.I rs
and
.B yytext
includes
.IR s .
Because a trailing
.B $
is implemented as
.BR /\en ,
the newline is part of
.B yytext
for such rules.
.PP
The
.B |
action does not share the next rule's action; the rule does nothing.
.PP
Indented code and
.B %{ %}
blocks in the rules section are not copied to the output.
.PP
.B yywrap ()
is always defined in the output, so a user-supplied
.BR yywrap ()
causes a duplicate definition.
There is no
.BR yylineno .
.PP
The generated scanner keeps all input in one 65536-byte buffer that is
never reused; input beyond that point is treated as end of file.
